140: Exact algebra: `code/part3/source_zero_real_trace.py`, with JSON and captured
141: output. Its certificate assigns no global return.
142: 
143: ## RT.5. The charged ruling-mapping-torus model
144: 
145: We compute a specified compact model, without identifying it with an end of
146: the completed family. Put
147: \[
148:  K=S^2_h\times S^3_q,\qquad
149:  \tau(h,q)=(q^{-1}hq,q),
150: \]
151: and form its mapping torus using this displayed first-to-second arrow.
152: It is the projectivization of the rank-two bundle over \(S^3\times S^1\)
153: whose transition is the same \(SU(2)\) map \(q^{-1}\). This follows on the
154: unit spinor: \(u\mapsto q^{-1}u\) has projective image exactly \(\tau\).
155: Thus no abstract bundle substitution is required. Denote the second Chern
156: number of this explicitly clutched bundle, in the chosen base orientation,
157: by \(c\). The clutch has degree one up to the displayed inversion, so
158: \(|c|=1\); reversing the first-to-second arrow changes its sign.
159: 
160: Let \(h=c_1(O(1))\) on this projectivization and orient its sphere fibres
161: complex positively. The actual projective-bundle relation and pushforward are
162: \[
163:  h^2+\pi^*c_2(E)=0,\qquad \pi_*h=1.
164: \]
165: The vertical tangent Euler class is \(2h\), and the base \(S^3\times S^1\)
166: is parallelizable. For the specified charge-a line \(O(a)\) we therefore get
167: \[
168:  k=-a^3c,\qquad \ell=-4ac,\qquad
169:  \frac{4k-\ell}{24}=-\frac{a(a^2-1)c}{6}.
170: \]
171: The dual line reverses all three numbers. In particular the magnitude of the
172: charge-two model index is one, whereas the charge-one index is zero.
173: These equations compute the entire mapping-torus bundle, including its line.
174: They do not identify its boundary orientation with the remaining source-zero
175: attachment.
176: 
177: There is an exact finite-cap input for that attachment, proved in
178: `part3_source_zero_annular_attachment_working.md`, EA.4. In the reduced
179: sector the Cartier translation is
180: \[
181:  U_2^{\rm Cartier}=r_{\rm target}^{-1}U_2^{\rm tensor}r_{\rm source},
182:  \qquad r=X(p-G),
183: \]
184: and on the root \(q_\infty=z^m\),
185: \[
186:  U_{\rm cap}=z^{-m}U_2^{\rm Cartier}=B_0/(2h),\qquad
187:  B=2\lambda h\,U_{\rm cap}.
188: \]
189: The actual extending \(B_0\) and deck-invariant h prove that this map extends
190: and commutes with the full finite cap action. Thus one forward B period
191: contains exactly the t-ruling to s-ruling arrow, the inverse of \(\tau\) in
192: the convention above. The inverse B period contains \(\tau\). This verifies
193: the primitive arrow and excludes an additional ruling interchange at this
194: cap. It still does not identify the oriented residual boundary with the model.
195: 
196: ## RT.6. An embedded trace in the cut source product
197: 
198: The map of RT.1 is the normalization of the selected divisor q=0 in the
199: marked simultaneous source. Its only identifications are the two disjoint
200: sections \(D_+\) and \(D_-\) over the selected critical curve. At v=0 the
201: first lies on the strict component and the second on E; their images are the
202: same selected z=1 node. The other z=-1 node has a single preimage, the
203: crossing point of the central components in the smooth surface F.
204: 
205: Choose a smooth function \(\eta:F\to(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)\) constant
206: and with distinct values on disjoint tubes of \(D_+\) and \(D_-\). Such a
207: function is obtained with two disjoint tubular cutoffs. Then
208: \[
209:  p\longmapsto(\nu(p),\eta(p))
210: \]
211: is a proper embedding of F into the compact cut source times an interval:
212: its derivative is injective by RT.1, and the only possible distinct points
213: with the same source image are separated by their height values. This
214: constructs the trace in that particular source product. Its normal bundle
215: is the oriented sum of the actual complex normal line of the source
216: immersion and the interval line. The complex normal line is
217: \(K_F\otimes\nu^*K_{\rm source}^{-1}\), by the determinant of the displayed
218: immersion. On the finite source chart the residue trivializes the latter
219: canonical factor. The divisor identity
220: \(K_F=O(-D_+-D_-)\) follows from
221: \(K_{\mathbb P^1\times D}=-2H\), the blowup term E, and
222: \(D_++D_-=2H-E\).
223: 
224: On the actual outer parameter circle, the two F9 endpoint lifts have the
225: same zero source-period gamma index and lie in a short interval with
226: distinct ordered endpoints. The explicit relative interval construction
227: of SQ.4a–c therefore identifies that boundary graph with this graph after
228: an isotopy fixed outside its source neighbourhood. This establishes the
229: boundary graph map. It does not extend the source translation cut through
230: the central flop: that separate cut is the outstanding gluing map.
231: 
232: ## RT.7. The two local charged charts and the remaining boundary gate
233: 
234: In the resolved associated node chart
235: \[
236:  U=S_0z,\quad T_0=zV
237: \]
238: the variables \((S_0,S_1,T_1,z,V)\) have auxiliary weights
239: \((1,1,-1,a-1,-a)\) and physical weights \((0,0,0,1,-1)\).
240: Fixing V=1 gives the effective weights
241: \((-1,-1,+1,+1)/a\) and residual \(\mu_a\).
242: For \(a>1\), fixing z=1 gives
243: \((-1,-1,+1,+1)/(a-1)\) on \((S_0,S_1,T_1,V)\), with residual
244: \(\mu_{a-1}\). Both are literal gauge calculations. A stability choice
245: does not automatically include both origins.
246: 
247: Writing \(f(a)=a(a^2-1)/6\), the exact numerical identity is
248: \[
249:  f(a)-f(a-1)=a(a-1)/2.
250: \]
251: It relates the charged local models to the ordered trace expression, but
252: does not specify which charged strata occur in a global boundary.
253: 
254: For orientation control, on the ordinary Cox cover conjugate its two
255: negative physical-weight coordinates. This preserves the real
256: eight-dimensional orientation. With radius increasing outward, the
257: circle-first orbit orientation is
258: \(-dr\wedge\Omega_{\mathbb{CP}^3}\). A deleted origin thus has
259: positive complex \(\mathbb{CP}^3\) boundary. The physical nonzero-vector
260: line on that boundary is \(O(-a)\); the \(O(a)\) line is its dual U.
261: Consequently the physical model index on that oriented boundary is
262: \(-f(a)\). This is a local orientation calculation only.
263: 
264: The proposed real cut uses the compact marked source, cuts its suspension
265: outside the short graph, and inserts the Cox moment cobordism in place of
266: the birational U cut. The associated node \(UV=q\) is required on the
267: overlap, with its actual line character and the actual polynomial q.
268: Fourfold's finite-cap arrow in RT.5 supplies the cap side of this cut.
269: The following two maps are still absent:
270: 
271: 1. The finite, Hausdorff real overlap of that moment cut with the full
272:    q=0 associated-node chart, including its actual stable subsets.
273: 2. The labelled boundary map from the chosen node resolution (the
274:    j=-1 vertical fan of SZ.15) to the requested source-zero fan and the
275:    resulting orientation of every residual boundary.
276: 
277: The nonproper infinite central quotient of SZ.29 is not used as a replacement
278: for either map. We retain the local orientations and the cap arrow above
279: while leaving both individual source-zero returns undecided.
